WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.960
He might not be the X-Men’s most famous
foe, but the Juggernaut has been around for

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.720
over 50 years, with a complicated history
that ties him to everyone from Spider-Man

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.890
to Professor X to Doctor Strange.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:16.570
If you want to know his story, here’s the
truth behind Cain Marko, the unstoppable Juggernaut.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:18.570
The Uncanny X-Brothers

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:23.670
Before the Juggernaut even appears on panel
in Uncanny X-Men #12, Professor Xavier fills

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.070
the X-Men in on his origin story.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.530
As a youngster, the smart and sensitive Charles
was bullied by his older stepbrother, Cain

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:31.530
Marko.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:35.710
They were rivals their whole lives, although
Xavier was able to get the upper hand when

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:37.579
his psychic powers manifested.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:42.190
When the pair happened across "the sacred,
lost Temple of Cyttorak,", however, Cain grabbed

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:47.179
a ruby from a strange-looking statue, and
it transformed him into the unstoppable behemoth

00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:48.879
known as the Juggernaut.

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:53.129
The cave collapsed and buried Marko, but it
turns out that “unstoppable” wasn’t

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:54.129
just a catchphrase.

00:00:54.129 --> 00:00:58.449
Years later, Marko returned to hunt down his
step-brother with a distinctive helmet which

00:00:58.449 --> 00:01:01.430
made him immune to Professor X's psychic powers.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:05.280
That first battle with the X-Men, like many
that followed, was all about getting that

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.689
helmet off, enabling Xavier to lay down the
psychic whammy.

00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:13.650
“I’m trying to attack his mind… but
I can't get through.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.790
His helmet protects his mind from probes”

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:17.869
The power of Cyttorak

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:23.990
It wasn’t mentioned in the early stories,
but in 1992’s Doctor Strange: Sorcerer Supreme

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:29.430
#44, Cyttorak was revealed to be a demon who
had been banished to another dimension.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:34.090
He's a being of destruction and chaos, which
is why he chooses Earthly avatars like Cain

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:38.600
Marko and grants them the power to cause untold
devastation in his name.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.970
In a 2015 storyline, Cyttorak's appearance
was redefined, mirroring the Juggernaut’s

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:48.219
traditional red armor and domed helmet by
making him a big red creature with a large

00:01:48.219 --> 00:01:49.219
domed head.

00:01:49.219 --> 00:01:54.030
By giving shape to the character and motivations
of Cyttorak and showing the Juggernaut as

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:58.000
his earthly avatar, Cain Marko’s actions
were given a whole new meaning.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.820
They were chaotic, to be sure, but they were
exactly as chaotic as Cyttorak wanted them

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:03.820
to be.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.869
Black Tom’s BFF

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:11.039
In Uncanny X-Men #101, Chris Claremont and
Dave Cockrum introduced one of the weirdest

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:16.020
partnerships in comics when the Juggernaut
teamed up with Black Tom Cassidy, the cousin

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.330
of the X-Man known as Banshee.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:23.010
In the first story, their alliance really
only existed to justify the Juggernaut's surprising

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:27.870
presence in Cassidy Keep, but the pair remained
allies for years, and it’s safe to say that

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.150
Black Tom got the better end of that deal.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:35.430
The Juggernaut is, after all, a legendarily
unstoppable supervillain who can level mountains

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.049
and bring down buildings with a single punch.

00:02:38.049 --> 00:02:42.410
Black Tom Cassidy, on the other hand, is more
of a schemer, and at one point suffered from

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.209
a disease that was turning him into a tree.

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:49.230
Not exactly the most threatening mutant, but
when your real power is being best friends

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:52.439
with the Juggernaut, you don’t really need
to be.

00:02:52.439 --> 00:02:54.319
Juggernaut versus everybody

00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:58.970
While he tends to square off against the X-Men,
the Juggernaut’s path of destruction isn’t

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.900
just limited to the mutant corner of the Marvel
Universe.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.450
After all, a huge guy who smashes through
walls and can't be stopped by any known force

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.840
is a useful sort of all-purpose villain to
have around.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.959
He met Doctor Strange for the first of several
battles in 1969, since getting his powers

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:18.940
from a demonic artifact made him a natural
villain for the sorcerer supreme.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:23.280
While the X-Men were taking a break in the
early 1970s, the Juggernaut fought the newly

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.480
blue Beast in Amazing Adventures #16, and
took on Marvel's other famous unstoppable

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.890
behemoth in The Incredible Hulk #172.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:36.239
He would throw down with Thor several times,
including a story that also introduced a team

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:40.560
of young heroes known as the New Warriors,
and even fought his share of bad guys during

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.360
a brief stint as a member of the X-Men.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.090
And of course, he’s slugged it out with
Deadpool on plenty of occasions.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:50.690
The Juggernaut's greatest battle, however,
might also have been the single best story

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:54.379
he ever appeared in: "Nothing Can Stop the
Juggernaut."

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:58.930
In that story, Juggernaut arrives in New York
City in an attempt to kidnap the psychic Madame

00:03:58.930 --> 00:03:59.930
Web.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:04.069
Spider-Man tries to stop him, but finds out
that the title of the story is pretty accurate.

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:07.830
Fortunately, stopping the unstoppable is what
Spider-Man does.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:12.799
For all of his spidery abilities, one of Peter
Parker’s most important traits is his unwillingness

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:15.970
to give up, no matter how hopeless things
might seem.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:20.380
Rather than trying to match the Juggernaut
physically, Spider-Man eventually outsmarts

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:25.760
his unstoppable foe by tricking him into burying
himself in 40 feet of wet cement.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.050
The greatest bar fight ever

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:32.710
The Juggernaut has met the X-Men on plenty
of different battlefields, but the most memorable

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.760
might be a dive bar in Manhattan.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:40.190
It happened in Uncanny X-Men #183, and was
set in the aftermath of the original Secret

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:41.190
Wars.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.730
Among other things, that crossover saw Colossus
falling in love with an alien who promptly

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:49.740
died, leading him to break up with his long-time
love interest Kitty Pryde once he returned

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:50.740
to Earth.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.330
Unfortunately for Colossus, Wolverine has
always been protective of Kitty, and decides

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:59.600
to take his Russian teammate out to a bar,
get him drunk, and then beat him up.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:05.190
He's spared the trouble, however, by the coincidental
appearance of an out-of-costume Cain Marko.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:09.680
Wolverine manipulates the two into fighting,
and then he and Nightcrawler stand back and

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.570
watch as the two super-strong giants have
one of the greatest barroom brawls of all

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:15.570
time.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:20.010
The entire establishment is destroyed in the
fight, although the Juggernaut surprises everyone

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:23.630
by donating a massive roll of cash to fund
its reconstruction.

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:28.080
He takes off, Colossus learns his lesson,
and Wolverine is satisfied with the night

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:29.080
out.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.110
X-Men: The Last Stand

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:36.660
The Juggernaut made his cinematic debut in
2006's X-Men: The Last Stand.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.480
With a very charitable 58 percent rating on
Rotten Tomatoes, that movie is commonly regarded

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:45.800
as a bit of a mess, partly because it introduces
a ton of characters from the comics without

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:48.540
taking the time to actually make them characters.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:53.490
The Juggernaut, played by Vinnie Jones, definitely
suffers from this problem, although there's

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:55.460
one pretty memorable scene.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:59.020
"Don't you know who I am?

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.020
I'm the Juggernaut, bitch!"

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:08.740
When Juggernaut is chasing Kitty Pryde through
a large building, Kitty uses her mutant powers

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:13.210
to pass through the walls, but the Juggernaut
simply smashes through them like the battering

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:14.780
ram that he is.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:19.710
And as cool as that action is, the Juggernaut
dropping a reference to a viral video featuring

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:24.590
a fan-dubbed episode of the X-Men cartoon
is even cooler.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:26.130
Deadpool 2

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:30.720
The Juggernaut's surprise appearance in Deadpool
2 wasn't advertised, but at this point the

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:31.780
word is out.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:37.150
Unlike The Last Stand, Deadpool 2’s Juggernaut
is an entirely digital creation, and gets

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:41.790
to have a massively over-the-top fight with
his old comics rival, Colossus.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.470
In the credits, Juggernaut is listed as playing
himself, but screenwriter Rhett Reese revealed

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:51.190
to Comic Book Resources that Juggernaut's
voice and facial capture are actually performed

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:54.130
by the same actor as Deadpool: Ryan Reynolds.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:58.840
Colossus, Negasonic Teenage Warhead, and Yukio
manage to knock the Juggernaut out at the

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:03.210
end of the film, but his return for future
sequels seems inevitable.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:05.640
After all, nothing can stop the Juggernaut.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:07.050
Thanks for watching!

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:09.560
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.690
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

